  "|The reason: the market often sweeps \u2014 pokes just past \u2014 one side of these session candle ranges, trapping eager retail traders. Once the stop orders resting there have been triggered, price snaps back the other way. That is a liquidity sweep .": "5d12c83c9c396f7e.mp3",
  "|The reverse: LH is a lower high and LL is a lower low. A run of lower highs and lower lows is a downtrend .": "c9854693c080c943.mp3",
  "|The role flip \u2014 old resistance becomes new support. The market remembers its battles!": "08819c379386d684.mp3",
  "|The rule that matters most: a pattern only counts at a level . A hammer in the middle of a range is noise. A hammer on a support level you marked in advance is information.": "9b12ffe7a04894ea.mp3",
  "|The rule: risk only a small fraction of your account on any one trade. Many traders risk just 1\u20132% per trade. At that size, even five losses in a row is a setback, not the end of your account.": "564583679f146824.mp3",
  "|The same applies at other scales: the previous week's high, low and midpoint; the Asia session high and low; the London session high and low.": "18b442ed8550fdda.mp3",
  "|The second is GC \u2014 Gold futures , trading near 3,350 an ounce in the simulator. It is usually calmer than NQ, but it can move sharply when the world gets nervous. Many real traders watch exactly these two.": "ce790e1c41283b5b.mp3",
  "|The second is revenge trading : taking a loss, getting angry, and immediately entering another trade to win it back. That is emotion, not strategy. After a loss, pause, review, and reset.": "e02f51579e59241b.mp3",
